<h2>The rival narratives</h2>
<p>The prosecution alleged that the petitioner maintained sexual relations with a girl of about fifteen — the age stated in the FIR — over a period of one to one and a half years on a false promise of marriage; that on 25 March 2026 he took her from her home and forcibly had relations with her; and that a co-accused confined the two in a room while others assaulted and threatened the informant when she protested. The State and the informant pressed the refusal to marry after a physical relationship as the gravamen.</p>
<p>The defence pointed to a five-day delay in the FIR, a medical report recording neither external injury nor spermatozoa, and — most significantly — the victim's own statement under the BNSS giving her age as seventeen and describing a four-day stay with the petitioner at Motihari, suggesting a continuing and consensual association. The petitioner, with a clean record, had been in judicial custody since 20 April 2026.</p>

<h2>What weighed with the Court</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Duration:</strong> the FIR and the victim's statement themselves disclosed a relationship running well over a year, not a sudden act.</li>
  <li><strong>Medical evidence:</strong> the allegations stood uncorroborated by the medical report on record.</li>
  <li><strong>Custody:</strong> over six months in judicial custody with trial yet to progress.</li>
  <li><strong>Antecedents:</strong> a clean record, protected by a verification condition before release.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<p>Bail followed on bonds of Rs 10,000 with two sureties of the like amount to the satisfaction of the designated court at Motihari, on conditions of cooperation with trial, a close relative standing as one bailor, appearance on every date with cancellation for two consecutive unexplained absences, and completion of antecedent verification within ten days.</p>

<h2>The adolescent-relationship problem in POCSO practice</h2>
<p>Cases at the border of majority — where the alleged victim is sixteen or seventeen and the record discloses a sustained relationship — occupy a difficult space in POCSO litigation. The statute admits no consent below eighteen, and nothing in a bail order erodes that rule. Yet bail courts must decide custody today, on a record pointing in two directions about age and voluntariness. Orders like this one resolve the tension pragmatically: liberty pending trial, hedged by conditions that protect the complainant's side — mandatory attendance, a family surety, and antecedent screening — while the age question is left to the documentary hierarchy of Section 94 of the JJ Act at trial.</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Issue</th><th>Bail stage</th><th>Trial stage</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Age of the victim</td><td>Discrepancies noted as a factor</td><td>Proved by the Section 94 JJ Act hierarchy of documents</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Consent</td><td>Voluntariness of association considered for custody only</td><td>Irrelevant if victim below eighteen</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Medical report</td><td>Lack of corroboration aids bail</td><td>Conviction can still rest on reliable testimony</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Nothing in a bail order forecasts the verdict. The presumptions under Sections 29 and 30 of the POCSO Act operate at trial once foundational facts are proved, and breach of any bail condition — above all contact with the victim's family — is the surest route back to custody.</p>
</div>
